What do you do? And what does Seattle Children's do as far as the data science

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:37.879
function that you work inside?

00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:46.440
Yeah, so Seattle Children's started over 100 years ago. And we've expanded from a small

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.599
hospital in Seattle to being the primary pediatric hospital for all of Washington, Idaho,

00:01:54.599 --> 00:02:00.680
Montana, and Alaska. So not the biggest population, but definitely the biggest geographic

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:09.160
area of any children's hospital, pretty much anywhere. And we got into data science fairly

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:16.839
early on for hospitals. This was in the mid-teens. And I've been working with, I guess,

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:23.559
data science is such a weird thing, because we all call it different things and mean different

00:02:23.559 --> 00:02:28.360
things, but I use it inclusively about any sort of advanced analytics, whether it's biostats

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:34.440
or machine learning or AI, whatever. But the machine learning part was fairly new for health

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:48.919
care in the mid-teens. And we started doing that, yeah, it was about 2015, 2016. And we

00:02:48.919 --> 00:02:58.360
gradually expanded to now we have a team of eight. And we have probably 90-odd people in our data

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:05.559
analytics community. So it's fairly large. Oh, and for those folks who are interested in sort of

00:03:05.559 --> 00:03:10.520
the analyst angle of things, I think we've got three positions open right now. So if you go to

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.199
seattlechildrens.org, and look for our careers page, and then type data into the search, you

00:03:16.199 --> 00:03:21.320
can see those. And connect me with me on LinkedIn, and I can talk more about it.

00:03:22.039 --> 00:03:28.039
That sounds amazing. Thank you for sharing. Maybe Isabel or Rachel can behind the scenes go

00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:32.199
find a career website somewhere, and we can share it in the chat with everybody.

00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:39.960
All right. I think that this is a great spot to ask one of my favorite questions that Natasha

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.559
usually asks, which is a day in the life question. What does a day in the life or a week in the life

00:03:45.559 --> 00:03:51.960
look like for you that could help us build context around the data science job function?

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.240
So that might be like what tools do you work in? Are you an R team? Are you a Python team?

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.679
Where does your data get stored? Do you deploy things? What do they look like? Do you build

00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:10.600
things for other users internally? Yeah, I'd love to hear. Well, the short answer is all of the above.

00:04:11.559 --> 00:04:17.559
I am primarily an R user. I've been using R since the aughts, back before I even knew what

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:24.920
the tidyverse was. But we have other folks who are primarily Python users,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:34.600
and we have our data warehouse hosted in Google Cloud Platform. And we're an Epic organization,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:42.440
so we also have the chronicles and clarity and all that stuff for the various data sources within

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:53.320
Epic. We do anything from building a plot for a doctor to setting up a machine learning pipeline

00:04:53.320 --> 00:05:02.359
to predict our inpatient census every few hours. An Epic for everybody is an EHR,

00:05:02.359 --> 00:05:07.720
which is an electronic health record, just so we make sure we get all of our acronyms

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.559
and stuff out there. I was wondering also if you might want to touch just a little bit on

00:05:13.559 --> 00:05:18.839
your background for us, because I think it is super interesting where you came from and how

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:24.040
you got how you navigated your way into this healthcare data science space, because you are

00:05:24.839 --> 00:05:34.839
an ecology guy, right? And that's where your stats background actually came from and got

00:05:34.839 --> 00:05:40.200
developed. Could you tell us just a little bit about your windy road to data science and healthcare?

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:49.000
Sure. My undergrad degree is in philosophy, of all things, and I decided towards the end of

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:55.720
my undergrad that I didn't particularly want to do philosophy professionally. So I got enough hours

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.359
to have a minor in natural resource management, and then I got a master's in forestry, and then

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:10.279
a PhD in environmental science. And after my PhD, I ran a wildlife refuge for about three or four

00:06:10.279 --> 00:06:19.000
years, and then after that, I became a research professor for about a decade. But along the way,

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:27.320
well, in ecology, you end up having to learn a lot of different statistical tools to address

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:35.000
all of the very questions that come up. And so, you know, starting with the basics, everything

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.920
from a t-test all the way up to crazy ordination and cluster analysis, and this was way back when

00:06:41.559 --> 00:06:49.959
when it was hard to do those things back in the 90s. And when I became a professional,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:56.760
a lot of my colleagues realized that I had these stat skills, and they didn't want to develop them.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.720
They're like, oh, let's ask Dwight. He'll do the stats for us. So I ended up learning a lot of

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:10.760
things on other people's projects. Like, for example, I helped with a soil microdiversity and

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:16.760
chemistry project in Costa Rica, where I got a free trip to Costa Rica, which I guess I didn't

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:22.679
need to do because I was just doing the computer work, but I appreciated the visit. And

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:31.640
yeah, and then I guess in 2011, I moved into health care. And the reason for that is that in the late

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:37.000
aughts, my son was born with some serious health problems. And we were coming over to Seattle,

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:46.200
basically weekly, to Children's Hospital, Seattle Children's. And after a while, I was like, you know,

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:52.279
after a while, I was like, you know, I have these quant skills, I would really like to use them

00:07:52.279 --> 00:08:06.359
to help kids like my son. So I took some SAS classes on the side. And because SAS was primarily

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:18.040
what was in healthcare at the time. And I learned SAS and got into got into a company called Group

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:23.160
Health, which has been bought out since then by Kaiser Permanente. And so it's Kaiser Permanente

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:31.640
Northwest. And so at the time, I really wanted to get into children. But even my son's doctor was

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:39.080
like, you don't want to work at Children's, our IT is terrible. And so, but I tried anyway,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.320
and back then you had to have three years of healthcare experience before they'd even consider

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:48.840
you. But I got into Group Health, and I worked at Group Health for three years and one week.

00:08:52.599 --> 00:08:58.039
As long as it took. When I when you told me that for the first time, I just about

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:03.640
was like jaw on the floor, because I know so many people who are like, yeah, my plan was,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:08.679
I'm going to go get this experience that I need to do this other thing. And then along the way

00:09:08.679 --> 00:09:13.400
of getting that experience, their focus would shift. And they would be like, Oh, well, I turned

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.200
into a different person in those three years. And then I went and did something else. But you had

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:23.479
real focus, you were like, I'm going to go get the experience that I need. And you still had a focus

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:29.479
of I'm going to go put my effort where my heart is and do this thing. And I think that that is

00:09:29.479 --> 00:09:34.679
absolutely amazing. I was kind of tearing up as you were talking. I don't think I'm the only one.

00:09:36.679 --> 00:09:44.599
And I think that this is a fantastic example of someone persevering through the I don't have the

00:09:44.599 --> 00:09:49.640
domain experience, which is a question we get a ton on the Hangout, right? Like people asking,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.159
well, I really am interested in finance, but I don't have any finance background,

00:09:53.159 --> 00:09:57.159
or I'm really interested in insurance or supply chain, I don't have any background, how do I do

00:09:57.159 --> 00:10:06.359
it? Do you have any advice on that from a career career perspective? From what you did? Did you

00:10:06.359 --> 00:10:10.679
just go like, okay, well, I need this healthcare experience, let me go find some place that fits

00:10:10.679 --> 00:10:16.760
this bill, that's not exactly what I what I want, but really kind of gets me in the door or that I

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:27.239
have just enough experience to do? Yeah. So what I found living from ecology to healthcare was that

00:10:27.239 --> 00:10:33.960
the questions are different, but the tools are the same. And so, you know, just earlier this week,

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:40.840
I was doing an ordination problem on healthcare data instead of ecological data. So all of these

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:48.840
skills that all of you have are transferable. And the trick is to demonstrate how you can apply

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:57.159
what you already know to where you want to move into. And so one of the ways I did that to move

00:10:57.159 --> 00:11:01.400
into healthcare is that when I got interviewed at group health, well, when I had several interviews

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:08.119
before I got into healthcare, but at group health in particular, I brought artifacts of my work.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:13.080
So it wasn't just a website, I actually had maps printed out of some of the map work I'd done.

00:11:15.559 --> 00:11:19.799
What else did I bring? I brought a few examples of analytics projects that I had done

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:28.039
on paper so that they could look at them right there. And my boss at group health later on said,

00:11:28.599 --> 00:11:34.119
you're the only person I've ever seen do that. And it just struck me as something you should do

00:11:34.119 --> 00:11:37.159
because you want to not just talk to people when you're interviewing, you want to

00:11:37.799 --> 00:11:45.559
show them what you can do. So I think of, even if it's like a pet project that you did for fun on

00:11:45.559 --> 00:11:50.840
the side, bring some sort of artifact to the interview. That's wonderful advice. Thank you

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:55.239
for sharing. I think I saw some people in the chat saying, I really needed to hear this, that

00:11:55.239 --> 00:12:00.200
you can really push through that. I don't have that domain experience. I promise that you can

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.919
everybody. I know that it's, it can be really intimidating to be looking at this big wall of,

00:12:05.479 --> 00:12:08.840
I need the experience to get the job, but I need a job to get the experience. And it's

00:12:09.559 --> 00:12:16.599
often so, so disheartening. But I would also refer you to like our episode of the Data Science

00:12:16.599 --> 00:12:20.280
Hangout with Hubert, who is in the crowd right now, who I'm about to call on, by the way,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:26.840
to ask a question live. Get ready, Hubert. Who was like a computer programmer, a computer

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:34.679
scientist, a math person who made friends in this space of research and transplant research and

00:12:34.679 --> 00:12:42.280
stuff like that. And said yes to, can you help me solve this computer specific problem? And then

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:47.320
moved into this space and didn't really have the background there before that either. But there's

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:53.400
this power when you make friends and you are putting yourself in a space in a community way.

00:12:54.359 --> 00:12:59.799
Okay. So I said I wanted to call on, on Hubert and I absolutely do. Hubert, you had a question

00:12:59.799 --> 00:13:05.159
in Slido. I will put it in the chat for everybody to see. And I also highlighted it to the top so

00:13:05.159 --> 00:13:16.359
that you can see it as well. Okay. So can you hear me? Yes. Hi Hubert. Hi Dwight. So my question,

00:13:16.359 --> 00:13:24.359
what was my question? Oh, there it is. Yeah. Are there data or data quality resource constraints

00:13:24.359 --> 00:13:29.719
that affect your work? I've worked with data from children's hospitals, various children's

00:13:29.719 --> 00:13:34.760
hospitals around the country. And it's obvious that some of them do not have some of the

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:41.640
informatics or other resources and constructing their data sets or quality of their data. Does,

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:49.719
does that impact you at Seattle Children's? Absolutely. Yeah. In so many different ways.

00:13:49.719 --> 00:13:54.840
One is just the end size. I mean, any single hospital is going to have a small

00:13:54.840 --> 00:14:02.760
in any way. And pediatrics tends to be smaller than adult hospitals. And so that definitely

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:08.039
impacts what we do. We find that we really don't have the end to do machine learning very much

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:13.799
on the clinical side, on the business side. Sure. I work primarily on the clinical side

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:25.159
and some other things missing data because patients will come in and out

00:14:26.599 --> 00:14:31.239
to our specialists. If they can't get a specialist in the time they want to go see

00:14:31.239 --> 00:14:35.320
somebody else and then maybe come back later. And so we have a gap in the record.

00:14:38.119 --> 00:14:42.200
Parents move all the time and their kids go with them. So they're lost to follow up.

00:14:46.599 --> 00:14:52.119
And then I guess the biggest one that gets me, and it's the same problem for healthcare anyway,

00:14:52.119 --> 00:14:58.840
anywhere is that we have longitudinal data where decisions made along the way,

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:06.760
change the trajectory. And that's so hard to both conceptualize and deal with statistically.

00:15:08.359 --> 00:15:16.840
Yeah. So many sounds like problems that look just like problems I've seen in other sectors too,

00:15:18.599 --> 00:15:23.880
with customer journey in retail spaces and things like that, where you just have these big gaps

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:28.840
between interactions and you don't know why you have a gap in interaction and there is no follow

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:32.119
up and you're never going to be able to answer a question. I always think it's interesting that

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:38.280
this is, there are so many examples of just how experience transfers to different places. And I

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:44.520
wanted to call out in the chat as well, Richard had said, kind of as we were on our last topic,

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:49.320
Richard will be joining us by the way, on the data science hangout on November 19th. He's from

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:55.239
Hennepin County, but he said, I've been on interview panels and we always like specifics,

