examples and show and tell. If it's general and vague, the panel might be also struggling with

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:10.359
that too. I wanted to add to this and say, so few people actually do go to the step of having an

00:16:10.359 --> 00:16:16.039
example that if you do, you are automatically putting yourself in the top 10% of people who

00:16:16.599 --> 00:16:22.840
just tried, because there are a lot of people who are like, I think everybody assumes that everybody

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:26.520
else is doing something that they're not actually doing. They're not actually putting the effort

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:31.960
forth. And you might assume, oh, those 400 people who clicked that thing on LinkedIn applied for

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:37.320
that job when really maybe only 20 followed through and actually applied for that job.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:44.359
And then maybe out of those 20, only one of them has a portfolio or an example or a link to click

00:16:44.359 --> 00:16:50.679
through to see work that they've done. So if you just decide to put the effort forward, you might

00:16:50.679 --> 00:16:57.080
be in the minority already. Thank you, Richard. We're looking forward to seeing you in November.

00:16:57.799 --> 00:17:01.719
All right, let's head back over to Slido and let's grab another question here.

00:17:03.479 --> 00:17:09.959
Because we have a question from Noor and Noor, there are a few here. Hold on. Wait a minute.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:17.160
Noor had three questions. I'm going to put one to the top here because you asked one about AI.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:20.040
And I think that's very interesting. I'm going to put it in the chat for everybody as well. I just

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:26.359
wanted you to know which one I was talking about. I'm sorry. I was really enthusiastic. We love your

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:33.640
prolific question asking. Anyway, this is regarding the question that unfortunately everyone gets.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:41.239
And I apologize for the clichedness. But we're unfortunately for better work in the age of AI,

00:17:41.239 --> 00:17:48.280
whatever that means. Have you felt a push to incorporate AI into your workflows from

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:55.319
stakeholders at the hospital or not? Maybe not. And what are some challenges and or pitfalls

00:17:55.319 --> 00:18:03.800
with that, especially with the kind of delicate data you would direct with? Right. Good question.

00:18:05.079 --> 00:18:17.319
So a few years ago when I think AI was at its peak of the hype cycle, I felt a lot of top-down

00:18:18.839 --> 00:18:26.920
not pressure, but not request either, more like a sentiment that, yeah, let's use AI. It's going

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:33.239
to solve all our problems. And it was exactly what I heard in the mid to late teens about machine

00:18:33.239 --> 00:18:41.000
learning. So I was like, OK, not a fad. Let's just see how this goes. And so the organization moved

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:47.880
forward with AI. And we have a private instance of Gemini. So we do have that on-prem. And it's

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:55.239
HIPAA compliant. So we can use it for patient data. And none of it goes back to Google.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.920
So that part's cool. But that's sort of where it stalled out.

00:19:02.599 --> 00:19:09.959
So we can access Gemini through the web interface with prompts. We can't do it programmatically

00:19:09.959 --> 00:19:13.959
yet. So it's like, hmm. So I cut and paste a lot of code.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:24.760
The pressure to use more AI, I think, has gone down a little bit as people realize that

00:19:27.079 --> 00:19:31.400
it does have the same problems in machine learning, that if you don't have a lot of in,

00:19:32.119 --> 00:19:39.319
it doesn't help and can even hurt. And then probably more

00:19:41.079 --> 00:19:45.880
concerning is that if you have too many conflicting advice to give the AI,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:52.199
the model is going to collapse back to the average. And if you're in health care, it's like,

00:19:52.199 --> 00:20:01.079
nobody's average. So we use it in a few areas in the hospital. Like we have something called

00:20:01.079 --> 00:20:08.359
clinical standard work. And a lot of hospitals have this. And what that is, is a set of

00:20:08.359 --> 00:20:16.760
instructions for doctors to follow when they have a suspected diagnosis. And it's basically

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:23.800
meant to be the best available evidence on treatment pathways. And you don't have to

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:29.239
follow it as a doctor. If you have reasons to diverge from it, you can. But we've put that

00:20:29.239 --> 00:20:37.160
into an AI now. So instead of slipping through thousands of pages of PDFs online, you can

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:42.760
actually query it and bring it up. And our AI interface will actually give you references

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:51.000
for each one. So it's not making stuff up in theory. But a lot of people are experimenting

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:57.479
with it in the hospital. That's the only thing that I know that's actually being used,

00:20:58.439 --> 00:21:00.040
other than us writing code with it.

00:21:01.479 --> 00:21:05.400
It sounds like the security part is very, very important in the health care space,

00:21:05.959 --> 00:21:13.319
making sure that things don't go back into a system to train, which is a struggle. But there

00:21:13.319 --> 00:21:20.359
are the different providers, different model providers, do work with people to be HIPAA

00:21:20.359 --> 00:21:24.839
compliant, as far as I can tell, and sign agreements. So if you have questions about that,

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.199
for sure, ask around in the Discord if you're in the health care space, because there are some

